1d:["$","p",null,{"children":["A driver must supply eight methods: ",["$","code",null,{"children":"post_init"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"has_with_results"}],",\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"update"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"get_part"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"inner_store"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"as_any"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"as_any_arc"}],", and\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"register_remove_callback"}],". Only the middle three are the ones you would\nguess. The other five are what makes composition work, and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"inner_store"}]," in\nparticular is where composition either succeeds or silently stops."]}]
1e:["$","$L1b",null,{"as":"h2","id":"the-key-space-is-not-just-digests","children":"The key space is not just digests"}]
1f:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"StoreKey"}]," has two variants: ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Digest(DigestInfo)"}]," and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Str(Cow<str>)"}],". Most\nof Forge deals in the first. The second exists because two subsystems\nneed to store things that are not content-addressed at all: the Build Event\nProtocol service, and the scheduler's awaited-action database."]}]
20:["$","p",null,{"children":["This has a consequence people hit and misread. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"StoreKey::into_digest()"}],"\nconverts a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Str"}]," key to a digest by BLAKE3-hashing the string, with the hash\nfunction hardcoded. That conversion is lossy and irreversible: a store that\nonly understands digests will accept a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Str"}]," key and file it somewhere\ndeterministic, but the original string is gone. The filesystem store handles\nthis more honestly by keeping the two in separate namespaces on disk (",["$","code",null,{"children":"s/"}],"\nfor string keys, ",["$","code",null,{"children":"d/"}]," for digests) so the two can never collide."]}]
21:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-brand/40 bg-brand-soft/40 text-foreground","role":"note","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-brand","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M12 16v-4M12 8h.01M12 22c5.523 0 10-4.477 10-10S17.523 2 12 2 2 6.477 2 12s4.477 10 10 10Z"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"Why a hardcoded hash here is fine"}],["$","p",null,{"children":"Elsewhere, a hardcoded hash function would be a correctness bug, because\nthe client picks the digest function per request. Here it is not: these\nkeys never cross the REAPI wire and are never compared against a\nclient-supplied digest. The hash is an internal placement function, not an\nidentity."}]]}]]}]
22:["$","$L1b",null,{"as":"h2","id":"the-digest-on-the-wire-is-a-claim-not-a-measurement","children":"The digest on the wire is a claim, not a measurement"}]
23:["$","p",null,{"children":"This is the single most important thing on this page."}]
24:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Forge does not derive a blob's digest from its bytes on upload."}]," The\nclient declares the digest in the ByteStream resource name, and the server\nstores the bytes under the declared key. See\n",["$","a",null,{"href":"https://github.com/toxicwind/forge/blob/v1.6.5/forge-service/src/bytestream_server.rs","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","data-source-link":"forge-service/src/bytestream_server.rs","data-source-ref":"v1.6.5","title":"forge-service/src/bytestream_server.rs at v1.6.5","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 align-middle font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground no-underline transition-colors hover:border-brand/50 hover:text-brand","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"12","height":"12","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"m16 18 6-6-6-6M8 6l-6 6 6 6"}]}],"ByteStreamServer"]}],".\n(The one exception is a zstd-compressed ByteStream upload, where the decoder\nalready has to read every byte and rejects a decoded stream whose size or\nhash does not match the digest.)"]}]
25:["$","p",null,{"children":["For plain uploads, the only thing that turns a claimed digest into a verified\none is a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"verify"}]," store somewhere in the graph, and both of its checks\ndefault to ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"off"}],":"]}]
26:["$","div",null,{"className":"relative overflow-auto prose-no-margin my-6","children":["$","table",null,{"children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"children":[["$","th",null,{"children":"Field"}],["$","th",null,{"children":"Default"}],["$","th",null,{"children":"What it costs when on"}]]}]}],["$","tbody",null,{"children":[["$","tr",null,{"children":[["$","td",null,{"children":["$","code",null,{"children":"verify_size"}]}],["$","td",null,{"children":["$","code",null,{"children":"false"}]}],["$","td",null,{"children":"Nearly nothing; a running byte count"}]]}],["$","tr",null,{"children":[["$","td",null,{"children":["$","code",null,{"children":"verify_hash"}]}],["$","td",null,{"children":["$","code",null,{"children":"false"}]}],["$","td",null,{"children":"One hash pass over every uploaded byte"}]]}]]}]]}]}]
27:["$","p",null,{"children":["A cache with no ",["$","code",null,{"children":"verify"}]," store in the write path is a cache that will store,\nand later serve, whatever a buggy or hostile client claims. That is not a\nForge-specific hazard (it is how the REAPI is specified), but the\ndefault being ",["$","code",null,{"children":"false"}]," means it is a decision you are making whether or not\nyou know it. ",["$","$L4d",null,{"href":"/explanations/correctness-hermeticity","children":"Correctness and hermeticity"}],"\ncovers what to do about it."]}]
