2b:["$","p",null,{"children":"is worth understanding, because three of its properties are not apparent from the config."}]
2c:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Size is checked eagerly, before a byte is read."}]," If the declared size is\nalready wrong at the point the request arrives, the upload is rejected\nwithout transferring anything."]}]
2d:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"The hash function is read per-request from the ambient context"}],", not from\nconfig. This is what lets one store serve both SHA-256 and BLAKE3 clients\nwithout duplicating the graph."]}]
2e:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Verification runs concurrently with the write, not before it."}]," The inner\nstore's ",["$","code",null,{"children":"update"}]," and the checker are driven together, so verification adds no\nserialised pass. The cost of that design is that a failing upload has already\nbeen partially written to the inner store when the failure is detected."]}]
2f:["$","p",null,{"children":["Size is enforced along three separate paths (overshoot, an exact hit that\nturns out not to be EOF, and undershoot at EOF), and the hash is compared at\nEOF. Every failure is ",["$","code",null,{"children":"InvalidArgument"}],", which is the correct code: the\nclient sent something that did not match what the client said it was sending."]}]
30:["$","div",null,{"className":"my-6 flex gap-3 rounded-xl border px-5 py-4 border-amber-500/40 bg-amber-50/60 text-foreground dark:bg-amber-500/10","role":"alert","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"20","height":"20","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"1.75","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","className":"mt-0.5 shrink-0 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"M12 9v4m0 4h.01M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0Z"}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1 [&>p]:my-0 [&>p+p]:mt-2","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-1 font-mono text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.12em]","children":"Nothing is re-verified on read"}],["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"VerifyStore::get_part"}]," is a bare pass-through. Verification is a\n",["$","strong",null,{"children":"write-side"}]," property only. A blob that entered the store before you added\nthe verify wrapper, or through a path that bypassed it, will be served\nwithout complaint forever."]}]]}]]}]
31:["$","$L1b",null,{"as":"h2","id":"composition-and-the-barriers-in-it","children":"Composition, and the barriers in it"}]
32:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"inner_store"}]," is how the system looks ",["$","em",null,{"children":"through"}]," a wrapper to find an\noptimisation opportunity, for example, to discover that the real backing\nstore is a filesystem store and a hardlink is possible."]}]
33:["$","p",null,{"children":["Most wrappers return ",["$","code",null,{"children":"self"}]," from ",["$","code",null,{"children":"inner_store"}],". That is not laziness; it is a\nstatement that looking through them would be wrong, because they transform\nthe bytes or the key. A ",["$","code",null,{"children":"compression"}]," store cannot let a caller hardlink its\ninner file: the inner file is not the blob, so most wrappers are\n",["$","strong",null,{"children":"optimisation barriers"}],", and only three forward: ",["$","code",null,{"children":"size_partitioning"}],",\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"shard"}],", and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"ref_store"}],", the three that route without transforming."]}]
34:["$","p",null,{"children":["The practical rule when composing: ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"put transforming wrappers as close to the\nbackend as you can"}],". A ",["$","code",null,{"children":"verify"}]," in front of a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"fast_slow"}]," is a barrier in\nfront of the fast path."]}]
35:["$","$L4e",null,{"children":"graph LR\ncaller[\"Caller<br/>(CAS service, worker)\"]\nsubgraph forward[\"Forward inner_store: routing only\"]\n  sp[\"size_partitioning\"]\n  sh[\"shard\"]\n  rs[\"ref_store\"]\nend\nsubgraph barrier[\"Return self: optimisation barriers\"]\n  vf[\"verify\"]\n  cp[\"compression\"]\n  dd[\"dedup\"]\nend\nfs[\"filesystem<br/>hardlink possible here\"]\ncaller --> forward\nforward --> barrier\nbarrier --> fs\nforward -. \"caller can see through\" .-> fs\n"}]
36:["$","p",null,{"children":"A caller reaching through the routing wrappers can discover the filesystem\nstore and hardlink from it. A caller reaching through a transforming wrapper\ncannot, because what is on disk underneath it is not the blob."}]
37:["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"StoreOptimizations"}]," is the vocabulary for the other half of this:\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"FileUpdates"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"NoopUpdates"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"NoopDownloads"}],", ",["$","code",null,{"children":"LazyExistenceOnSync"}],",\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"SubscribesToUpdateOneshot"}],", capabilities a store advertises so callers can\nskip work rather than discovering at runtime that it was unnecessary."]}]
38:["$","$L1b",null,{"as":"h3","id":"fast-slow-writes-through-and-deliberately-re-uploads","children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"fast_slow"}]," writes through, and deliberately re-uploads"]}]
39:["$","p",null,{"children":["With the default ",["$","code",null,{"children":"fast_direction"}]," and ",["$","code",null,{"children":"slow_direction"}],", writes go to both\ntiers concurrently and ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"both must succeed"}],". It has no write-behind and no\neventual consistency window: an upload that returns OK is durable in the slow\ntier."]}]
3a:["$","p",null,{"children":["The surprising part is existence. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"fast_slow"}],"'s ",["$","code",null,{"children":"has_with_results"}],"\n",["$","strong",null,{"children":"intentionally never consults the fast store"}]," (unless the slow tier is a\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"noop"}]," store, in which case only the fast tier is checked). It asks the slow\ntier, then counts any slow-tier writes still in flight. A blob that is\npresent only in the fast tier is reported absent, which causes the client to\nupload it again, and that upload is what gets it into the slow tier. The\napparent inefficiency is the mechanism that stops the fast tier from becoming\na place where data can be lost."]}]
3b:["$","p",null,{"children":"Reads use a leader/follower scheme so that a hundred simultaneous misses for\nthe same blob produce one fetch from the slow tier rather than a hundred.\nFollowers wait up to one minute for the leader before giving up and fetching\nfor themselves; that timeout is what stops a stuck leader from wedging every\nreader behind it."}]
3c:["$","$L1b",null,{"as":"h3","id":"dedup-is-a-chunking-index-and-it-has-opinions","children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"dedup"}]," is a chunking index, and it has opinions"]}]
3d:["$","p",null,{"children":["A ",["$","code",null,{"children":"dedup"}]," store splits blobs with content-defined chunking (FastCDC), stores\nthe chunks, and writes an index under the ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"original"}]," key. Two large blobs\nthat share most of their content share most of their chunks."]}]
3e:["$","p",null,{"children":["Two things to know before enabling it. First, chunk hashing is ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"hardcoded to\nBLAKE3"}]," regardless of the digest function the request used, which is\ncorrect for the same reason as ",["$","code",null,{"children":"StoreKey::into_digest"}],", since chunk hashes\nare placement, not identity, but it does mean a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"dedup"}]," store's internal\nlayout is unrelated to your clients' digest function."]}]
3f:["$","p",null,{"children":["Second, a corrupted index reports itself inconsistently: ",["$","code",null,{"children":"has()"}]," returns\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"NotFound"}]," while ",["$","code",null,{"children":"get_part"}]," returns ",["$","code",null,{"children":"Internal"}],". If you are debugging a store\nwhere existence checks and reads disagree, a damaged dedup index is the\nfirst thing to suspect."]}]
40:["$","$L1b",null,{"as":"h2","id":"two-things-that-are-not-what-their-filenames-say","children":"Two things that are not what their filenames say"}]
