4a:["$","$L4f",null,{"type":"multiple","children":[["$","$L50",null,{"title":"Should I turn on verify_hash?","id":"should-i-turn-on-verify-hash","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"On the write path of anything shared between people, yes. The cost is one\nhash pass over uploaded bytes, concurrent with the write rather than\nserialised before it. The thing it buys is that a digest in your cache\nmeans what it says. On a purely local single-developer cache the\ncalculation is different, because you are the only client and you are not\ndefending against yourself."}]}],["$","$L50",null,{"title":"Why is my fast tier getting the same blob uploaded twice?","id":"fast-tier-duplicate-uploads","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["That is ",["$","code",null,{"children":"fast_slow"}]," working as designed: existence checks skip the fast\ntier, so a blob present only there is reported missing and re-uploaded,\nwhich is what lands it in the slow tier. The alternative would be a cache\nthat reports a blob present when the only durable copy of it does not\nexist."]}]}],["$","$L50",null,{"title":"Does wrapping a store in compression break hardlinking?","id":"compression-and-hardlinks","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["Yes, and it must. Hardlinking works by handing the worker the store's own\nfile; a compression store's file is not the blob. That is why it returns\nitself from ",["$","code",null,{"children":"inner_store"}],"; the barrier is the correct answer, not a\nmissing optimisation."]}]}],["$","$L50",null,{"title":"Can I point the CAS and the action cache at one store?","id":"cas-and-ac-same-store","children":["$","p",null,{"children":"No, and the config parser rejects it before anything starts. They hold\ndifferent value types under keys from the same space, so sharing a store\ncorrupts both."}]}]]}]
